# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# goclient build output (compiled with `go build .` / cross-compiled for deployment) --
# extensionless on macOS/Linux, so the *.exe pattern above doesn't catch these
/lastwar-client
/lastwar-client-*

# Session config / device identity -- these belong in the user's home directory
# (~/.lastwar_goclient_*), never in the repo. This is a defensive backstop, not the
# real safeguard, in case one ever ends up here by mistake. Covers the session
# config file plus the four persisted device-identity files written by
# identity.go's stateFilePath() family (device_id, username, gameuid, loginkey).
*.lastwar_goclient_session.json
.lastwar_goclient_*
config.local.json

# Raw packet-capture artifacts from docs/capturing-and-decoding-traffic.mdx's documented
# capture-and-decode workflow (tcpdump -> reassemble_stream.py -> -decode-stream). These are
# unencrypted wire bytes of a real Login request/response -- more sensitive than the session
# config above, since they carry loginKey/airKey/deviceId/shumeiBoxId in the raw, same risk
# class, same defensive-backstop reasoning.
*.pcap
*.pcapng
capture.pcap
*_c2s.bin
*_s2c.bin

# Node / Mintlify docs tooling (docs/package.json)
node_modules/
